  "translation": {
    "literal": "On a thousand mountains the flying birds have vanished; on ten thousand paths there is no human footprint. A lone boat, an old man in a straw cape and bamboo hat, fishing alone in the snow of the cold river.",
    "poetic": "Over a thousand hills, no bird flies; on ten thousand paths, no footprint is left. In a lone boat sits an old man in cape and hat, fishing alone in the snow on the cold river."
  },
  "notes": [
    {
      "id": "first-characters",
      "kind": "interpretation",
      "title": "A hidden message: 千萬孤獨",
      "body": "The first characters of the four lines, 千 · 萬 · 孤 · 獨, can be read together as \"a thousand, ten thousand, lonely, alone\". Whether the poet planned it or not, the poem opens with huge numbers and shrinks to one fisherman. The first two lines widen to an empty landscape and the last two zoom in on one small figure."
    },
    {
      "id": "exile",
      "kind": "history",
      "title": "Written in exile",
      "body": "Liu Zongyuan (773–819) was banished to Yongzhou (in modern Hunan) after the failure of the 805 reform movement. Readers see the lone fisherman, who stays in the freezing river while the world has gone indoors, as a portrait of the poet's own stubborn integrity."
    },
    {
      "id": "rhyme",
      "kind": "rhyme",
      "title": "Rhyme: 絕 · 滅 · 雪",
      "body": "Lines one, two and four end in 絕 (jué), 滅 (miè) and 雪 (xuě). All three were entering-tone (入聲) syllables with a short, clipped ending. The ending is still audible in Cantonese: 絕 zyut6, 滅 mit6, 雪 syut3. The abrupt stop suits the poem's cold, hard mood."
    },
    {
      "id": "tones",
      "kind": "tone",
      "title": "Entering tones",
      "body": "Mandarin lost the entering tone, but the three rhyme words and 一 · 獨 · 笠 · 釣 all kept it in Tang speech. Reciting the poem in Cantonese or Hokkien lets you hear a final consonant that Mandarin dropped."
    }
  ],
